review_comments.body:
No, it's not architecture-specific. This 2 MiB deliberately mirrors the VMM's own hard-coded pmem check in hypervisor/vmm/src/device_manager.rs:2788:

if size % 0x20_0000 != 0 {
  return Err(DeviceManagerError::PmemSizeNotAligned);
}
// The memory needs to be 2MiB aligned in order to support hugepages.

0x20_0000 is a fixed 2 MiB literal, not gated by any #[cfg(target_arch)], so it's the same value on x86_64 and aarch64. 2 MiB is the PMD-level huge-page size on x86_64 and on aarch64 with 4 KiB base pages — the configs this VMM's pmem/DAX path targets — which is why the VMM hard-codes it. By construction the Go side then produces exactly the size the VMM's check accepts, so it can't be under- or over-aligned relative to the VMM.
If you'd prefer, I can add a short comment pointing at device_manager.rs:2788 as the source of truth so the two constants don't silently drift.

**Missing SPDX license identifier.** Both `disk.go` and `util.go` in the same package include the full header:

```
// Copyright (c) 2026 Tencent Inc.
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
```

The test file has only the copyright line. Automated license compliance scanners (e.g., REUSE, FOSSA) may flag this inconsistency.

**Defensive safety guard can discard a legitimate larger file size.** When `fi.Size() > estimatedSizeBytes` (unlikely but possible with a corrupt or unexpected `resize2fs -M` result), the condition here is false, so `haveActualSize` stays `false` and `fsSize` remains `estimatedSizeBytes`. The code then truncates to `alignUp(estimatedSizeBytes, 2MiB)` which could be *smaller* than the actual file, cutting into live data.

Since `alignUp` only rounds up, there's no risk in unconditionally trusting `fi.Size()`. Consider removing the `<= estimatedSizeBytes` constraint:

```suggestion
	} else if fi.Size() > 0 {
		fsSize = fi.Size()
		haveActualSize = true
		if fi.Size() > estimatedSizeBytes {
			log.G(ctx).Warnf("file size %d exceeds estimate %d (unexpected), using actual size", fi.Size(), estimatedSizeBytes)
		}
	}
