review_comments.body:
**Architecture: Sequential per-endpoint delivery blocks the channel consumer**

The worker loop calls `deliver_to_endpoint().await` directly inside `rx.recv()`, serializing all deliveries for a given endpoint. If delivery to endpoint A takes 2 seconds (slow server + retries), every subsequent event queued for A is delayed by at least that long. During that window the bounded channel fills up and `try_send` drops events.

Consider spawning each delivery as a separate `tokio::spawn` so the consumer can drain the channel immediately:

```rust
while let Some(message) = rx.recv().await {
    match message {
        Message::Event(event) => {
            let payload = match serde_json::to_vec(&event) {
                Ok(p) => p,
                Err(e) => { error!(…); continue; }
            };
            tokio::spawn({
                let c = client.clone();
                let ep = worker_endpoint.clone();
                async move { deliver_to_endpoint(&c, …).await }
            });
        }
        Message::Flush(reply) => { let _ = reply.send(()); }
    }
}
```

This decouples channel consumption from I/O and gives per-endpoint concurrency.

